            <p class="lead">
                A retired teacher from Vancouver paid $4,200 per implant at her local clinic. She flew to Beijing instead and got the same Straumann implant, placed by a board-certified prosthodontist, for $1,350 each. Total bill including flights, hotel, and two weeks of recovery? Less than half what she'd have paid at home for a single implant. This is not an isolated story.
            </p>

            <h2>Section 1: Why Dental Implant Costs in China Are Dropping in 2026</h2>

            <p>Here's something the dental industry doesn't advertise loudly: China has been running a national volume-based procurement program since 2023 that has slashed implant prices by roughly 50%. The government negotiates directly with manufacturers on behalf of public hospitals, and those savings pass through to patients.</p>

            <p>Straumann — the Swiss premium implant maker — reported in February 2026 that it is growing globally but facing "China procurement uncertainty" as this bulk-buying mechanism reshapes the market. That uncertainty is good news for foreign patients: it means hospitals are competing harder for international clientele, which drives quality up and prices down.</p>

            <p>The Grand View Research China Dental Tourism Market report (October 2024) projects steady growth through 2033, with cost competitiveness being the primary draw. Meanwhile, Korean dental device manufacturers are actually expanding into other Asian markets because China-based demand has softened — a signal that Chinese domestic supply is saturating and foreign patients are the growth target.</p>

            <h2>Section 2: Current Dental Implant Costs in China vs. Worldwide (2026)</h2>

