            <h2>Section 8: Risks, Contraindications & Who Should NOT Go to China for Implants</h2>

            <div class="risk-box">
                <h3>Medical Contraindications — You May Not Be a Candidate If:</h3>
                <ul style="margin: 10px 0 0 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Uncontrolled diabetes:</strong> HbA1c above 8% significantly increases implant failure risk. Get your levels stable before considering overseas surgery.</li>
                    <li><strong>Active smoking:</strong> Smokers have 2-3x higher implant failure rates. Most surgeons require 2-4 weeks pre- and post-operative smoking cessation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Severe osteoporosis:</strong> While mild bone loss is manageable with bone grafting, severe osteoporosis may make implant integration unreliable.</li>
                    <li><strong>Immunocompromised state:</strong> Including active chemotherapy, recent organ transplant, or autoimmune disease requiring immunosuppression.</li>
                    <li><strong>Heavy teeth grinding (bruxism):</strong> Without proper management (night guard), implants can fail from excessive occlusal forces.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Situational Risks</h3>

            <p><strong>Follow-up continuity:</strong> The most legitimate risk with any overseas dental work is what happens when you get home. Chinese hospitals provide detailed records in English, but your local dentist needs to be willing to co-manage your case. Confirm this before you travel.</p>

            <p><strong>Implant brand continuity:</strong> If you choose a specific brand (e.g., Straumann), confirm your home country dentist can service that brand. Some regional distributors only carry certain lines.</p>

            <p><strong>Air travel after surgery:</strong> Most surgeons recommend waiting 5-7 days after implant placement before flying. Plan accordingly — this means booking accommodation for at least a week post-surgery.</p>

